commit.message:
fix(harness): stream a host tool that throws as a tool-error (#25195)

## Background

When a host tool's `execute` throws, `maybeExecuteHostTool` submits `{
error: String(err) }` to the runtime with `isError: true`, and the
runtime echoes it back as a `tool-result` event with `isError: true`.
`translateStreamPart` kept that mark only for provider-executed calls
and calls from an earlier slice (#18902), so the echo reached
`fullStream` as a successful `tool-result` whose output is `{ error }`.
The UI message stream sent `tool-output-available`, and the step listed
the call in `toolResults`. In the same run, `onToolExecutionEnd`
received a `tool-error`, and the approval path, which emits through
`enqueueHostToolOutcome`, streamed a `tool-error` for the same failure.
#18902 listed this path as future work. #25194 has the details and a
standalone reproduction.

## Summary

- `translateStreamPart` now turns every failed tool result into a
`tool-error`, and marks it `providerExecuted` unless its call is a host
tool's. A failed host tool streams as a `tool-error` with
`providerExecuted: false`, so `onError` still formats it when tool
errors are redacted, as #18902 required.
- Its `error` is the echoed result, which is what the runtime received
(`{ error: 'Error: …' }`), with the session working directory stripped
like in every other part. Host-only details stay with
`onToolExecutionEnd`: a thrown error's other properties, or the detailed
error of a failed context validation, never reach the stream. Without
redaction, the UI message stream sends it as `{"error":"Error: …"}`, the
same text that the Claude Code bridge gives the model, in line with
#24980. The approval and pause paths still stream the error object that
the host recorded; both now mark the failure as one.
- For a host tool, whether the call failed comes from the result the
host submitted (`isError`), not from the runtime's echo, because
adapters differ in what the echo's flag means. fx sets it from the model
output, so it also flags a denied call and a successful result whose
`toModelOutput` returns an error; those stay `tool-result`s, as before.
pi's echo of a failed host tool carries no flag at all; the call is
still marked failed. The submission is recorded before the adapter's
`submitToolResult` is called, so this holds whether the echo arrives
before or after it resolves (fx echoes inside `submitToolResult`).
- Unchanged: what the runtime and the model receive, failed
provider-executed tools (still `providerExecuted: true`), results for
calls from an earlier slice, and successful host results.
- Tests: the `translateStreamPart` test and the two `runPrompt` tests
that pinned the old projection now expect one `tool-error` that is not
marked provider-executed. New tests use a session that echoes the result
after it was submitted, as a runtime bridge does:
- A thrown `Error` with an extra property and a path under the working
directory: the stream carries only the stripped text the runtime
received, and the UI message stream sends it as `tool-output-error`.
  - A failed context validation: no context value reaches the stream.
- A `toModelOutput` that throws: one `tool-error`, and the stream
closes. On main it never closes, because building the response messages
calls the throwing `toModelOutput` again for the `tool-result`.
  - `onError` formats the error when tool errors are redacted.
- What the runtime's flag says doesn't matter for a host tool: a denied
call and a successful result with an error model output stay
`tool-result`s when the runtime flags them, also when it echoes inside
`submitToolResult`, and a failed call stays a `tool-error` when the echo
has no flag.
- An approved host tool that fails after the approval round trip, with a
session that echoes the submitted result (suggested in #25194): one
`tool-error`. The harness streams that failure itself and drops the
runtime's echo of a call it has already settled.

On main's code, 8 of these tests fail. The three runtime-flag cases that
expect a `tool-result` pass there, as they should, and so does the
approval case, because main already drops that echo. On this branch, the
approval case fails if the echo is no longer dropped, because the
failure then streams as a second `tool-error`. Without the override, all
four runtime-flag cases fail. Two narrower changes to it are caught as
well: recording the submission only after `submitToolResult` resolves
fails the echo inside `submitToolResult`, and counting the host's flag
only when the echo agrees fails the unflagged failure. The redaction
test fails if host errors are marked provider-executed.
- The docs already describe this behaviour (stream parts and UI messages
"carry an error with the same generic message" in Tools; `onError`
formats the errors of failed host tool calls in UI), so they are
unchanged.
- In `packages/harness`: `pnpm test` passes (node 802, edge 721), and
`pnpm type-check` and `ultracite check` are clean.

## End-to-End Verification

Flow: the public `HarnessAgent` API (`createSession`, `stream`,
`toUIMessageStream`) with a scripted `HarnessV1` runtime. The runtime
calls a host tool and echoes the submitted result back with its
`isError` flag, as a runtime bridge does. The host tool throws. Limit:
not run against a live runtime such as Claude Code in a sandbox; the
scripted runtime stands in for its echo.

The script is the one from #25194, saved outside the checkout. Its first
line is the issue's note for running it against the npm release; here it
runs against the checkout's build. Both runs build the checkout and run
the script from `examples/harness-e2e-tui`, where `@ai-sdk/harness` and
`ai` are workspace links:

